  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Western Tailed-Blue (Cupido amyntula) is a small butterfly belonging to the Gossamer-winged family, characterized by delicate wings and subtle coloring. Despite its name, this species has expanded its range eastward and can now be found in parts of New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Tail-like Extensions",
      "description": "Look for small, thread-like tails projecting from the hind wings, which give this butterfly its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Wing Color",
      "description": "Males display bright blue uppersides while females are gray-brown; both have white undersides with black spots and orange patches near the tail",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "This is a tiny butterfly with a wingspan of only 0.75 to 1.25 inches, making it easy to overlook",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Western Tailed-Blues prefer open, sunny areas with low vegetation including grasslands, meadows, and disturbed areas where their host plants like lupines and other legumes grow.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Males often engage in 'hilltopping' behavior, gathering at the tops of hills to locate females, making certain elevated areas reliable spots for spotting these butterflies.",
